<!--
  Pixelpond Wear OS Micro Icons — fish
  Watch Face Format (WFF) usage snippet.

  WFF reference (image usage + tinting):
    https://developer.android.com/training/wearables/wff/images

  IMPORTANT — Android resource naming:
    Copy a PNG into your WFF project under  res/drawable-nodpi/  (recommended for
    pixel art so the system does not resample it) or  res/drawable/ , and rename it
    to a valid Android resource name: lowercase a-z, 0-9, underscore only.
      png/24/fish.png   ->   res/drawable-nodpi/fish_24.png
    Reference it by name WITHOUT the extension:  resource="fish_24"

  Pick the export that matches your slot:
    16px slot -> fish_16   |   20px -> fish_20   |   24px -> fish_24   ...
  These are static, complication-safe icons. For animated, data-reactive
  decorations use the separate "Wear OS Motion Charms" pack.
-->

<!-- 1) Basic placement: a 24x24 icon at (x, y) on the face. -->
<PartImage x="158" y="120" width="24" height="24">
  <Image resource="fish_24" />
</PartImage>

<!-- 2) Theme color: ship a PRE-TINTED export from the tints/ folder.
        tints/pond/fish-24.png  ->  res/drawable-nodpi/fish_pond_24.png  -->
<PartImage x="158" y="120" width="24" height="24">
  <Image resource="fish_pond_24" />
</PartImage>

<!-- 3) Ambient-safe note:
        The icon is a single 1-bit-friendly ink shape on transparency, so it stays
        crisp in ambient (low-power) mode. For ambient, prefer the white or one-bit
        export on a dark dial, keep it at >=16px, and avoid scaling between sizes —
        use the export that already matches the target size for hard pixel edges. -->
